<!-- 4. MANUFACTURING STANDARDS AND REGULATORY COMPLIANCE -->
<section class="section" id="4-manufacturing-standards-and-regulatory-compliance">
  <h2>4. Manufacturing Standards and Regulatory Compliance</h2>
  <p>Automotive aerosol manufacturing is subject to strict environmental, safety, and METAS transport certifications. Facilities handling flammable propellants like LPG must operate under explosion-proof conditions and deploy automated leak detectors. Label declarations must clearly state flammability hazards, VOC levels, and disposal protocols. The core regulatory frameworks include:</p>
  <p>
                    Manufacturing automotive aerosols is a high-stakes endeavor that requires strict adherence to safety and quality protocols. Unlike standard liquid bottling, aerosol filling involves handling pressurized gases and highly flammable liquids, necessitating specialized facilities with explosion-proof equipment and advanced ventilation systems. Compliance with Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) is not just a regulatory hurdle; it is a foundation for product reliability.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Regulatory frameworks vary by region but generally focus on flammability labeling, Volatile Organic Compound (VOC) limits, and pressure vessel safety standards. In the global market, meeting these standards is a prerequisite for export and authority. Startups must invest in robust quality control systems that test every batch for pressure stability, leakage, and spray performance to ensure consumer safety and brand integrity.
                </p>
                
      <div class="image-slot">
